{
  "id": "global-scale",
  "title": "Global scale",
  "summary": "Two regions, geo-DNS routing and cross-region replication.",
  "lesson": {
    "problem": "Users in Europe wait 150 ms or more for every round trip to servers in the US, and one region failing takes the whole product down.",
    "why": [
      "Run the stack in several regions. Geo-DNS (or anycast) answers each user with the address of the nearest healthy region, so most requests stay local.",
      "Data has to be in both places. Here the US holds the primary database and Europe a replica: local reads are fast, but writes still cross the ocean to the primary.",
      "Replication between regions is asynchronous, so Europe can briefly see stale data. Multi-primary setups avoid the slow writes but must resolve conflicting updates."
    ],
    "takeaways": [
      "The speed of light is a real constraint: keep users near their data.",
      "Cross-region replication means choosing between consistency and latency.",
      "Regions are also failure domains; DNS can fail traffic over to another region.",
      "Data residency laws (such as GDPR) may decide where some data is allowed to live."
    ]
  },
